#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5fba78-066d-56fb-b5ac-6733fa0349bd" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying and Dividing Negative Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "0c43effb-b65a-503b-b56a-aac7d499ba0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4867" :question/problem "The average temperature in Helsinki, Finland, in January $2017$ was $-5.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$. If the average temperature (measured in degrees Celsius) in Yakutsk, Russia, in the same month was eight times lower, what was the average temperature in Yakutsk in January $2017$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the temperature in Yakutsk, we multiply the temperature in Helsinki by $8$. This gives\n\n$$\n(-5.5^{\\circ}\\, \\textrm{C}) \\times 8 = -44^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average temperature in Yakutsk in January $2017$ was $-44^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623e299c-b14a-5fba-aa7e-3fe556be7c6e" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Operations on Negative Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "2111bc1e-b9e1-58b1-873f-d8f94b8446ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-5100" :question/problem "Martin has an overdraft facility authorized by his bank. His bank balance at the beginning of the month was $-\\[math] 550$.\n\nTo reduce his debt with the bank, Martin deposited some money into his account. After making the deposit, his new balance was $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ of the initial balance. Then, at the end of the month, Martin made a withdrawal of $\\[/math] 40$.\n\nWhat was Martin's bank balance at the end of the month?" :question/worked-solution "To determine Martin's bank balance after he made the deposit, we multiply the initial balance by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(-\\[math] 550) \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} =-\\[/math] 220\n$$\n\nMartin's bank balance was updated again when he made a withdrawal. To determine the final balance, we subtract the amount of money he withdrew from $-\\[math]220$. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\[/math] 220 - \\[math] 40 =-\\[/math] 260\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martin's bank balance at the end of the month was $-\\[math] 260$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "413e7e81-2cd5-5954-835e-aa1da05e7dc5" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Negative Fractions and Mixed Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "ed21898f-dfc1-543a-bf57-313c6e94dd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9770" :question/problem "The temperature in a chamber that holds chlorine is $-24\\dfrac{1}{3}{^\\circ}\\textrm{F}$. It's known that chlorine boils at $\\dfrac{6}{5}$ the chamber's temperature measured in degrees Fahrenheit. What is the boiling temperature of chlorine?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the boiling temperature of chlorine, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n-24\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n-24\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n-24\\dfrac{1}{3} = -\\dfrac{24\\times 3 + 1}{3} = -\\dfrac{73}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-\\frac{73}{3}) \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} &= -\\frac{73 \\cdot 6}{3 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{73 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{73 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{73 \\cdot 2}{\\cdot 5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{146}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{146}{5} = -29\\dfrac15\n$$\n\nTherefore, chlorine boils at\n\n$$\n-29\\dfrac15{^\\circ}\\textrm{F}\n$$"}]}
